  "difficulty_reason": "Counted unit: original Theorem 6.1 of the article (source label thm_main_1, source0.tex lines 2146--2190, printed as Theorem 6.1 and stated in the same words as Theorem 1.2 in the introduction), the Sadovskii vortex family theorem with its existence, regularity and orbital-stability parts, delivered with the author's own complete 80-line proof at source0.tex lines 2192--2271. H2: the proof is not a routine derivation from the delivered lemmas. For the stability part the solver must set up the sharp-energy variational problem, recognise that a failure of orbital stability produces a sequence of initial data whose energy converges to the maximization value with impulse and $L^p$-norm bounded by $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$, keep that property along the flow through the Yudovich conservation law, and then extract from Theorem 5.2 a maximizer that contradicts the assumed separation from the maximizer set; the $p=\\infty$ case additionally requires the decomposition of the data into a maximizer and a remainder, the comparison of the two energies through the sharp energy inequality, and the recovery of the impulse of the remainder from the convergence of the impulse of the maximizer part. The existence, geometry and regularity parts must be assembled from the Euler--Lagrange identities, the Pohozaev identities, the compact-support and touching lemmas and the regularity lemma with its optimality remark. This chain of concentration-compactness analysis for a steady vortex of the 2D Euler equations is well beyond ordinary PhD qualifying level.",
